Setting: Grothendieck studies a group scheme M_{ρ,σ} (over a base S, ultimately over Z) attached to a pair of matrices ρ, σ in GL_2 (the 'équation des lacets' situation). Its pieces are:
- T_ρ, the torus centralising ρ; its special subgroup ST_ρ (det = 1) and S'T_ρ ((det)^2 = 1);
- T_σ, the torus of σ, and N'_σ, its normaliser-type extension by the 'conjugation' character ε_2;
- the subgroup M_{ρ,σ}(0) ≅ T_0 × T_ρ × N'_σ;
- the subgroup 𝔖^+_{ρ,σ} ≅ SG ≅ SL_2;
- the identity component M°_{ρ,σ}, the kernel of (χ, ε_2, ε_3).
The previous page found a large central subgroup (formulas (47)-(52)).

This page specialises to the 'modular case' (53): det ρ = det σ = 1, with ρ = [[0,1],[-1,1]] of order 6 and σ = [[0,1],[-1,0]] of order 4. These are the standard elliptic generators of SL_2(Z), with ρ^3 = σ^2 = -1.

In this case ρ ∈ ST_ρ and σ ∈ ST_σ. Both therefore lie in the centre of M°_{ρ,σ}. Using the three canonical morphisms (55):
- i_ρ : ST_ρ → M(0) ⊂ M;
- i_σ : SN'_σ → M(0) ⊂ M;
- i_G : SG ≅ 𝔖^+ → M;
one gets a homomorphism (54): Z/6 × Z/4 → Centre(M°_{ρ,σ}), (i,j) ↦ i_ρ(ρ^i) i_σ(σ^j).

When 2 is invertible on S, its image contains the image of μ_2 × μ_2 × 1 from (48), i.e. the two copies of -1 (ρ^3 and σ^2). Under the same hypothesis the homomorphism is injective. In characteristic 2 it is not, since -1 = 1 there.

Over the absolute base Z, in the modular case, he records (56):
- 𝒯_{ρ,σ}(Z) ≅ T_ρ(Z) × N'_σ(Z) ≅ Z/6 × D_4. Here T_ρ(Z) = Z[ζ_6]^* ≅ Z/6, and N'_σ(Z) = Z[i]^* ⋊ Gal ≅ D_4 of order 8.
- M_{ρ,σ}(Z) ≅ 𝒯_{ρ,σ}(Z) ⋉ SL(2,Z), a semidirect product.
- In this semidirect product Z/6 × D_4 acts through the character ε_2 : D_4 → Z/2 = {1, τ_∞}. τ_∞ acts on SL(2,Z) by conjugation by diag(-1,1), the standard outer automorphism of SL_2(Z) that makes PGL_2(Z) = Aut(PSL_2(Z)).

Purpose: to compute centres and radicals of the symmetry group of the loop-equation data, as a preparation for the homomorphism Θ : M → G and its kernel SR° = ST_ρ × ST_σ on the next page.
